WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.650
Okay, let's talk about the economy today.
Um, Donald Trump has been touting the current

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:07.150
economy as the best ever.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:12.590
Trump has been bragging about how strong the
economy is in general. He says it's the best

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.330
it's ever been for black Americans, the best
that's ever been for Hispanic Americans. And

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:22.890
he usually points to unemployment rates and
the stock market being high. And yet at the

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:28.070
same time, 40% of the population is still
struggling just to pay their basic bills every

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:32.960
single month, which is more than a hundred
million people in this country. How is it

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:38.019
possible that we have the best economy ever?
And 40% of the population is struggling to

00:00:38.019 --> 00:00:42.440
pay their bills? And to really understand
this, you actually have to zoom out because

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.019
it's bigger than Trump. Of course, when Trump
says something like, this is the best economy

00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:53.619
ever. It's meaningless political hyperbole.
But there's a deeper analysis to be done here

00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:58.239
that is beyond left right politics, or at
least it's beyond Donald Trump. So let's do

00:00:58.239 --> 00:00:59.239
it.

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:03.449
It's true that the economic expansion that
the US has seen, dating back to the beginning

00:01:03.449 --> 00:01:09.479
of Barack Obama's presidency is on paper the
longest economic expansion in the history

00:01:09.479 --> 00:01:15.640
of the United States of America. That's true.
As defined as as as we define expansions and

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:21.939
still we know that a huge portion of the country
can't even afford an unexpected $400 emergency

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:27.770
expense. How is it possible that both are
true? The stock market is at record highs.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:33.630
The unemployment rate is historically at it's
lows, and yet there are a record number of

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:38.979
people who are 90 days or more behind on car
payments, which is a concerning indicator

00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:43.920
that can predict economic trouble and that
number continues to climb every single month.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:51.640
That's not good. How can there be on c a signals
on paper that things are so good and at the

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.130
same time, nearly half the country can't pay
its bills?

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:58.560
And the answer effectively boils down to one
word. If we need to boil it down to one word

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.820
and that word is inequality. It's a little
simplistic, but in a sense it's all about

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:09.190
that one word. Inequality. I'll explain the
economic expansion. Dating back to Obama has

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:15.220
been hugely unequal. The gains in the stock
market that we've seen dating back to Obama

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:21.680
are a hugely concentrated in a very small
portion of the population that owns the vast

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.630
majority of the stock. So for a huge number
of people, even though on paper the stock

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:32.370
market is way, way, way up, it does very little
for a huge number of people that don't own

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:39.290
any or almost any shares of stock wage growth
wage has gone mostly to the upper tier net

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:45.709
worth growth has gone mostly to the upper
tier, stagnant wages on the lower tier.

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:51.629
So again, on paper you can come up with broad
averages that look good or at least look okay.

00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:55.500
But for a huge portion of the country, cost
of living has continued to go up, but their

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.599
inflation adjusted wages have not. So they
can't pay their bills even though on paper

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:05.299
they should be okay. Unemployment is low and
the stock market is high, just as very little

00:03:05.299 --> 00:03:08.489
for a lot of the people living in the United
States of America. So when you go all the

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:16.410
way back to 1989 30 years ago, the bottom
half of the country is less wealthy today.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:22.390
Inflation adjusted than they were 30 years
ago. This is stunning given the economic growth

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:27.900
that in total the United States has seen in
the last 30 years and the explanation is inequality

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:34.030
or specifically unequal growth in almost every
aspect of the United States economy. And the

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:40.030
problem with this is that the economy is in
a sense hanging by a thread, even though so

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.360
many numbers look good.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:47.870
As Donald Trump talks about, there are like
100 million people in this country for whom

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:54.010
even a mild economic setback in the broader
economy can push them into a completely unsustainable

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:58.290
financial situation. This could include a
bankruptcy, this could include having to rack

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:03.530
up crushing credit card debt at high interest.
This could include a home foreclosure or an

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:08.280
eviction from a place that they rent, vehicle
repossession, and of course remember that

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:14.810
in the back of our minds is the possibility
that a medical bill could bankrupt us completely.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:19.420
Our health insurance system fundamentally
weakens the economy because of the system

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:24.910
that we have. People who are even in an okay
financial position are still at risk of losing

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:29.420
everything because of unexpected medical bills
that may not be covered by their insurance.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:33.440
That's how the system works. That's how the
country works, which sheds a lot of light

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:38.690
on the reality that our health insurance system
is an economic liability beyond just being

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:40.350
a moral and ethical liability.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:44.790
So the thing about all of this is that because
these things are so political, each president,

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:48.431
each party, they always want to take credit
for any wins they can take credit for. They

00:04:48.431 --> 00:04:52.980
want to shirk responsibility for any of the
losses that they can successfully shirk responsibility

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:59.400
for that. We end up unable to have the real
serious conversation. As a result, this is

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.560
actually bigger than Republicans and Democrats
and now it's true. It's not totally apolitical.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.100
It's true that historically Democrats are
better than Republicans in terms of stock

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:14.750
market returns, but that doesn't affect everybody
equally. It's true that historically Democrats

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:20.100
are better than Republicans in terms of the
average unemployment rate during their presidency.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.470
But that doesn't actually tell us whether
people are making a living wage or whether

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:28.460
they have to work multiple jobs in order to
make ends meet by the standards we use.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:32.580
Democrats are better than Republicans, but
unfortunately those standards fall very, very

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:37.210
short and these are just bigger that don't
even enter into the political election cycles

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:41.910
that we have of two, four and six years, two
years for the House of Representatives, four

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:46.470
years for the president and six years for
the u s Senate respectively. Automation and

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:51.260
technological unemployment is one of those
issues. Inequality is another one of those

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:56.370
issues. Education costs are another issue
that around the margins you will hear Poe,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:00.660
politicians talk about, but nothing serious
has been done to deal with that. The health

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:05.300
care system. Fortunately it's entering more
into the political discussion, particularly

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:10.770
since the, uh, since 2010 and Obamacare, uh,
and Bernie Sanders mainstreaming the idea

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:17.160
of Medicare for all in 2016. But this is why
you hear the economy is the best ever, and

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:22.740
there are actually some numbers that sort
of seem to reflect that in some way, but yet

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:27.950
almost half the country is hanging by a thread
that's not going to change. Listen, if the

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:34.620
stock market, if the Dow goes to 30,000 still
a huge portion of the country will be at risk

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:40.190
of a $400 unexpected expense, throwing their
financial situation into total disrepair.

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:46.050
Uh, you are not going to change that if the
unemployment rate goes from 3.8 to 3.7%. Okay?

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:52.300
These are bigger systemic issues and inequality.
If I had to sum it up in one word, tells us

00:06:52.300 --> 00:08:00.060
a lot about why this is the case. Let me know
what you think. I'm on Twitter at d pacman.

